 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training time is comparable to standard 3DGS but requires joint optimization of the selector and transformation networks, potentially increasing per-step cost [S1]. Inference time is reduced as fewer Gaussians are rendered [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Inference memory scales with the target ratio $e$, allowing deployment on devices with limited VRAM [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Experiments on ZipNeRF, MipNeRF, and Tanks&Temples scenes demonstrate effectiveness in balancing rendering speed and visual quality [S1]. Specific PSNR/SSIM metrics or FPS numbers are not provided in the sources [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Inference time is proportional to the number of selected Gaussians, which is $eN$. The selection and transformation steps add negligible overhead compared to rendering [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is proportional to the number of selected Gaussians, $eN$. The selector and transformation networks are lightweight [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Experiments on ZipNeRF, MipNeRF, and Tanks&Temples scenes demonstrate effectiveness in maintaining rendering performance while reducing model size [S1].
 *   **Strengths:**
-    *   Single model supports arbitrary compression ratios without retraining [S1].
-    *   Adapts to diverse hardware constraints (smartphones, VR) [S1].
-    *   Avoids the need for separate fine-tuning runs for each ratio [S1].
+    *   Single model can be deployed on diverse hardware constraints (smartphones, VR headsets) [S1].
+    *   No additional fine-tuning required for different ratios [S1].
+    *   Adapts to arbitrary ratios during inference [S1].
 *   **Limitations:**
-    *   Requires joint training of additional modules, increasing training complexity [S1].
-    *   Performance at extreme compression ratios (e.g., 1%) may be challenging due to the difficulty of learning robust selection and transformation policies [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When deploying 3DGS on multiple devices with varying computational capabilities and avoiding the cost of maintaining multiple pruned models [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When maximum possible quality is required for a single fixed hardware configuration (standard 3DGS or post-hoc pruning with fine-tuning might yield better results for that specific ratio) [S1].
+    *   Requires joint training of selector and transformation modules, which may increase training complexity compared to standard 3DGS [S1].
+    *   Performance at extreme ratios (e.g., 1%) may still be challenging, though improved over heuristics [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When deploying 3DGS models on devices with varying computational resources and avoiding the need for multiple model versions or retraining is critical [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When the highest possible quality at very low ratios is required and the overhead of joint training is prohibitive [S1].
 
 ## Background
-FlexGS was introduced by Hengyu Liu, Yuehao Wang, Chenxin Li, Ruisi Cai, Kevin Wang, Wuyang Li, Pavlo Molchanov, Peihao Wang, and Zhangyang Wang from The Chinese University of Hong Kong, University of Texas at Austin, and Nvidia [S1]. The paper "FlexGS: Train Once, Deploy Everywhere with Many-in-One Flexible 3D Gaussian Splatting" was published in CVPR 2025 (arXiv:2506.04174v1, June 4, 2025) [S1]. It addresses the limitations of standard 3DGS in terms of memory overhead and the rigidity of previous compression methods that require separate fine-tuning for each target compression ratio [S1].
+FlexGS was introduced by Hengyu Liu, Yuehao Wang, Chenxin Li, Ruisi Cai, Kevin Wang, Wuyang Li, Pavlo Molchanov, Peihao Wang, and Zhangyang Wang from The Chinese University of Hong Kong, University of Texas at Austin, and Nvidia [S1]. The paper "FlexGS: Train Once, Deploy Everywhere with Many-in-One Flexible 3D Gaussian Splatting" was published in CVPR 2025 (arXiv:2506.04174v1, June 4, 2025) [S1]. It addresses the limitations of standard 3DGS in terms of memory overhead and the lack of adaptability for different devices in previous compression methods [S1].
 
